WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:02.429
Film Courage: What does writing a marketable
screenplay mean?

00:00:02.429 --> 00:00:03.929
Wendy Kram, Consultant for screenwriters,
directors, production companies: Great question,

00:00:03.929 --> 00:00:06.050
probably one of my favorite questions.

00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:15.510
I often say that you can have a great idea,
super-high concept (I think I referenced previously)

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:21.269
I’ve heard story ideas and I go “Oh my
gosh, that is great!

00:00:21.269 --> 00:00:27.649
I want to option it before I even read it
and then I read it and it’s not well-executed.

00:00:27.649 --> 00:00:33.550
So if you have a great concept that’s not
well-executed, chances are that it will not

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:34.550
sell.

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:40.219
If you have a high-concept that is well-executed,
you have a much better chance of it getting

00:00:40.219 --> 00:00:41.489
sold.

00:00:41.489 --> 00:00:49.609
Conversely, you can have a project that is
totally not high-concept but if it is beautifully

00:00:49.609 --> 00:00:54.649
executed, it then becomes marketable.

00:00:54.649 --> 00:01:01.420
So for example if we take a movie like JUNO
(and I’ll define high-concept).

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:07.000
High-concept is THE HANGOVER, a bunch of guys…you
know from the movie poster…I mean a lot

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:13.470
of your audience knows what high-concept is
but there are those who don’t and it’s

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:20.230
terminology we use a lot and it’s basically
where the concept is very big and immediately

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:23.280
recognizable from the movie poster.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:30.150
So THE HANGOVER and it’s guys and we all
know it’s THE HANGOVER, it’s in Vegas

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:37.520
(you can tell from the movie poster) and they
look like they’re a wreck and it’s 48

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:40.610
hours before a bachelor is to walk down the
isle.

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:47.270
At his bachelor party they go to Vegas and
his friends lose him and they don’t have

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:52.300
any recollection of what happened the night
before and have 48 hours to find him before

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:53.300
the wedding.

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:56.100
That’s one example of big concepts.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:03.720
SPEED is a big concept, anything that is based
on a brand like TRANSFORMERS it has an immediately

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:07.340
recognizable built-in audience, brand value.

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:11.820
So the opposite of that would be a movie like
SLUMDOG MILLIONAIRE.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:19.310
In face, most of the movies that win the Oscars
are not high-concept.

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:23.450
SLUMDOG MILLIONAIRE, MILLION DOLLAR BABY,
okay if you were to pitch this…I’m going

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:27.870
to do not justice to the movie but if you
were going to think about it but trying to

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:33.860
get a studio, this is a story about a girl
who comes from a trailer park and she wants

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:42.150
to be a boxer and she’s mentored by this
older dude and she winds up injuring herself

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:52.090
to the point where she becomes paralyzed from
the waist down and basically asks him to do

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:54.100
something drastic, so he does.

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:55.560
Okay, who would buy that?

00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:02.580
SLUMDOG MILLIONAIRE, it’s a great story,
great movie.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:12.200
No known American actor but you did have an
A-list director (Danny Boyle).

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:18.980
So that is definitely what helped get it made
and it was also based on a book.

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:25.000
SLING BLADE another magnificently written
script and some of these movies would definitely

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:26.950
be harder to get done today.

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:37.260
But you take JUNO, family drama, teenage girl…I
mean really, that you used to be like an after

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:44.050
school special (no disrespect), more of a
TV movie than a feature that would build such

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:47.280
a big following.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:54.690
This year LADY BIRD, that’s not a high-concept
right-of-passage with the young girl but it

00:03:54.690 --> 00:04:01.400
was so well-written and it resonated so much
with people, the mother-daughter relationship,

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:04.930
the notion of growing up in a small town.

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:14.800
So what makes a script marketable, the script
like JUNO and with LADY BIRD, the quality

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:22.979
of the writing and the freshness of the characters,
the truthfulness of the characters, that then

00:04:22.979 --> 00:04:26.129
becomes marketable.

00:04:26.129 --> 00:04:32.559
That comes down to me saying write your best
work.

00:04:32.559 --> 00:04:39.509
I don’t know any executives (other people
may), I don’t know any executives who have

00:04:39.509 --> 00:04:45.229
optioned a script if they didn’t care of
the dialogue, the writing, I mean structure

00:04:45.229 --> 00:04:48.870
maybe is the least offensive.

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:56.960
It can be a good idea and maybe the structure
is not great but the characters are compelling,

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:01.110
the situation’s compelling and the dialogue
is good.

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:06.319
And I just don’t know anyone who buys scripts
if those elements aren’t there.

00:05:06.319 --> 00:05:12.330
So therefore I think if you are going to make
a profession as a screenwriter, it’s not

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:14.559
an easy profession at all.

00:05:14.559 --> 00:05:22.270
You have to roll up your sleeves and really
get in there and I’m working right now with

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:31.770
someone who just graduated from film school
and his writing is really good.

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:36.400
There are some elements that I think needed
to be much more pronounced, what the underlying

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:40.999
theme is and the purpose of the journey and
some things that didn’t track with the characters

00:05:40.999 --> 00:05:44.969
but there’s a lot of talent there but I
didn’t think it was ready to quite take

00:05:44.969 --> 00:05:50.830
out yet but he’s doing some work now and
he’ll get there.

00:05:50.830 --> 00:06:00.620
I mean there’s a lot of talent and there’s
a lot of writing that really isn’t very

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:10.969
strong and I think the biggest death to a
writer is to not be able to take notes well

00:06:10.969 --> 00:06:13.770
and I don’t mean just any notes.

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:21.040
To be open to constructive feedback and to
want to put in their dues.

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:28.259
So I think that’s the biggest killer as
well as trying to please too many people and

00:06:28.259 --> 00:06:33.800
not listening to your own inner voice and
I just say it in terms of marketability.

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:42.020
What gets agent’s attention, producers,
studio networks is that you’ll hear a strong

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:43.020
voice.

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:49.830
That there’s something really fresh about
a person has a point of view, that the character

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:57.050
writing…Quentin Tarantino is such a strong
voice and I would say Greta Gerwig has a strong

00:06:57.050 --> 00:07:02.240
voice, we could go on but…

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:11.740
Film Courage: And even going back to LADY
BIRD (just briefly) we went to see THE POST

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:14.930
and unfortunately the movie didn’t play
so we all had to leave the theater [and while

00:07:14.930 --> 00:07:19.870
waiting to leave] I spoke to a man who was
maybe in his late 50’s and he said he loved

00:07:19.870 --> 00:07:20.939
LADY BIRD.

00:07:20.939 --> 00:07:25.919
So here is someone who you really wouldn’t
think he would even be the audience for it

00:07:25.919 --> 00:07:29.810
but he said [he loved it] and he was recommending
it to the people he was with and we were talking

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:30.810
about it.

00:07:30.810 --> 00:07:33.309
So you can see how he somehow resonated with
that voice.

00:07:33.309 --> 00:07:39.999
Wendy Kram: I mean the truthfulness of that
mother-daughter relationship, which wasn’t

00:07:39.999 --> 00:07:47.360
pretty…how they got in each other’s faces
and talked to each other but it was truthful

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:54.310
and we know that they did love each other
and it was so touching toward the latter part

00:07:54.310 --> 00:08:00.750
of the movie when the mother who was really
kind of tough with her daughter and the daughter

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:05.419
didn’t feel like she really loved her (or
even liked her) and then we saw that she was

00:08:05.419 --> 00:08:09.280
trying to write a letter to her and crumbled
it up like 30 times.

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:15.410
That’s another form of love, versus this
would be the boring way of doing it and not

00:08:15.410 --> 00:08:19.849
so truthful to the human experience would
be “Well, you know I love you.

00:08:19.849 --> 00:08:24.499
Maybe I’m hard on you but maybe I’m doing
it for your own good or maybe I have my own

00:08:24.499 --> 00:08:28.919
issues so I’m not able to love that well.”

00:08:28.919 --> 00:08:34.139
Versus the simple, beautiful act the she was
crumpling up letters because she wanted to

00:08:34.139 --> 00:08:36.210
find the write words and couldn’t.

00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:40.300
That says so much more than anything that’s
on-the-nose and obvious.

00:08:40.300 --> 00:08:41.550
Film Courage: Right.

00:08:41.550 --> 00:08:46.760
And the whole part of coming from a smaller
town, even though it’s California’s capitol

00:08:46.760 --> 00:08:52.529
but there is small-town feel to Sacramento
for anyone who has driven through it and wanting

00:08:52.529 --> 00:08:54.430
to like really re-invent herself.

00:08:54.430 --> 00:08:59.399
She didn’t want to go by her given name,
she wanted to be a writer on the East Coast…it

00:08:59.399 --> 00:09:05.680
wasn’t okay to be a writer from California
and wanting to re-invent herself and I think

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:09.709
everyone can identify with that.

00:09:09.709 --> 00:09:18.860
Wendy Kram: Versus…that’s really wonderful
writing versus I want to be a writer, I’m

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:23.660
tired of being in this small town, I want
to reinvent myself.

00:09:23.660 --> 00:09:33.649
That’s such a good example of why some people
say when they get feedback about not to write

00:09:33.649 --> 00:09:36.190
exposition “What do you mean?

00:09:36.190 --> 00:09:39.639
People can talk about, what you don’t like
people to be direct?”

00:09:39.639 --> 00:09:45.860
Sure, there’s a time and place and sometimes
people are direct but most of the time they’re

00:09:45.860 --> 00:09:51.630
circling, trying to figure out their lives,
who they are and so on and that was a very

00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:54.639
beautiful example of that.

00:09:54.639 --> 00:09:55.410
Film Courage: Yeah…I agree.

